      "statement": "For the full independent driving law gamma=(Gaussian zeta0 product Gaussian zeta1) product (Gaussian array G0 product Gaussian array G1), and mu=nu product gamma, set a=exp(-h/2), sigma=sqrt(1-exp(-h)), MR=M+(1-exp(-h))*d, B=6MR+3eps^2+3eta*d and C=2+(1+log((1-c)^(-1)))/(-log c), d=finrank(E). There exist the same measurable exact proximal map p, actual stopped output q and natural index N, with p(y)+eta grad V(p(y))=y, ||q(y)-p(y)||<=eps and run(grad V,eta,eps,y,N(y)+1,y)=some(q(y),N(y)+1) for every y. Define P0=aP+sigma zeta0, Y0_i=X+t_i P0, Z0_j=grad V(q(Y0_j)+sqrt(eta) G0_j), Y1_i=Y0_i-sum_j omega_ij Z0_j, Z1_j=grad V(q(Y1_j)+sqrt(eta) G1_j), and Phi=(X+h P0-sum_j positionWeight_j Z1_j, a(P0-sum_j momentumWeight_j Z1_j)+sigma zeta1). Phi is measurable and gives a Markov kernel K(s)=gamma.map(z -> Phi(s,z)). For every i both gradient squares are integrable, with E_mu||grad V(Y0_i)||^2<=2MR and E_mu||grad V(Y1_i)||^2<=4MR+2S^2 B. Both actual counts N(Y0_i)+1 and N(Y1_i)+1 are integrable; their respective expectations are at most C[1+log(1+sqrt(2MR)/eps)] and C[1+log(1+sqrt(4MR+2S^2 B)/eps)].",
      "formula": "M_R=M+(1-e^{-h})d,\\quad B=6M_R+3\\varepsilon^2+3\\eta d,\\quad A_0=2M_R,\\quad A_1=4M_R+2S^2B,\\quad \\mathbb E_\\mu[N(Y_i^k)+1]\\le C_c[1+\\log(1+\\sqrt{A_k}/\\varepsilon)],\\quad k=0,1.",
