          "title": "Identify the successful queries used by both parents",
          "text": "The implemented-phase theorem supplies p,q,N and the complete phase map/kernel. The Picard-center theorem supplies another successful stopped q and N along with its moment conclusions. At every y, deterministic successful-execution uniqueness equates both returned pairs. Projection of that equality and natural-number cancellation identify q and N pointwise, so the center bounds now concern exactly the kernel witnesses. The exact proximal witnesses p need not be identified.",
          "formula": "\\operatorname{run}(y,N(y)+1,y)=(q(y),N(y)+1)=\\operatorname{run}(y,N\\prime(y)+1,y),\\quad q=q\\prime,\\ N=N\\prime.",
          "lean": "ProximalExecutionIdentity.successful_query_unique; congrArg Prod.fst; congrArg Prod.snd; Nat.add_right_cancel"
        },
        {
          "title": "Use both actual Picard-center moments",
          "text": "Apply the existing normalized-Hessian moment theorem under the full actual law using the derived refreshed state budget, full Gaussian G0 coordinates, supplied node bounds and row l1 bound. It gives the first-center bound 2MR, the first queried-gradient bound B, and the second-center bound 4MR+2S^2B. In particular neither actual-center gradient-square integrability nor its numerical bound is a premise.",
          "formula": "\\mathbb E\\|\\nabla V(Y_i^0)\\|^2\\le2M_R,\\quad\\mathbb E\\|Z_j^0\\|^2\\le B,\\quad\\mathbb E\\|\\nabla V(Y_i^1)\\|^2\\le4M_R+2S^2B.",
          "lean": "PicardCenterMoment.picard_center_gradient_moment"
        },
        {
          "title": "Bound the same actual query count in each layer",
          "text": "The C2 gradient and all finite phase-center maps are measurable. Apply the same-implementation expected-work theorem to each actual center under mu with the newly derived square-gradient integrability. Its count is N(Y)+1, including the final successful test. Since C>=0 and eps>0, monotonicity of square root and logarithm replaces the actual gradient moment by each derived numerical upper bound. Count integrability is a conclusion, and no different-law cost comparison is used.",
          "formula": "M_R=M+(1-e^{-h})d,\\quad B=6M_R+3\\varepsilon^2+3\\eta d,\\quad A_0=2M_R,\\quad A_1=4M_R+2S^2B,\\quad \\mathbb E_\\mu[N(Y_i^k)+1]\\le C_c[1+\\log(1+\\sqrt{A_k}/\\varepsilon)],\\quad k=0,1.",
          "lean": "RealizedProximalWork.realized_proximal_expected_work; Real.log_le_log; mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_left; gcongr"
        },
        {
          "title": "Return the same full phase and both per-node costs",
