  "reconstruction": {
    "text": "Let E be a complete finite-dimensional real inner-product space equipped with its Borel measurable structure, and let I be any finite index type. Let V:E->R be C^2 and kappa be a nonnegative real with 1<=kappa. Assume for every x,v in E that\n  kappa^(-1)||v||^2 <= D^2 V(x)[v,v] <= ||v||^2.\nFix real eta,c,epsilon with 0<eta<=c<1 and epsilon>0. Let nu be a probability measure on E x E, and fix xstar in E with grad V(xstar)=0. Fix h,M,S>=0, and assume that the function (x,r) -> ||x-xstar||^2+||r||^2 is integrable under nu with integral at most M. Let t:I->R satisfy |t_i|<=1 for every i, and omega:I x I->R satisfy sum_j |omega_ij|<=S for every i. Let b,d:I->R be arbitrary deterministic real arrays, denoting positionWeight and momentumWeight.\nWrite D=dim_R E, a=exp(-h/2), sigma=sqrt(1-exp(-h)), G for the centered identity-covariance Gaussian probability measure on E, gi=product_I G, gamma=(G x G) x (gi x gi), and mu=nu x gamma. Thus, under mu, an incoming state (x,r) of law nu is independent of mutually independent standard Gaussian vectors u,v and independent standard Gaussian arrays xi,xi-prime. Define the exact constants\n  MR=M+(1-exp(-h))D,\n  B=6 MR+3 epsilon^2+3 eta D,\n  C=2+(1+log((1-c)^(-1)))/(-log c).\nThen there exist measurable p,q:E->E and N:E->N such that for every y in E,\n  p(y)+eta grad V(p(y))=y,\n  ||q(y)-p(y)||<=epsilon,\n  run(grad V,eta,epsilon,y,N(y)+1,y)=some(q(y),N(y)+1).\nThe exact-real interpreter returns none at zero fuel. With positive fuel it evaluates g(x), sets next=y-eta g(x), and returns (x,1) if ||next-x||<=(1-eta)epsilon. Otherwise it recurses from next with one less fuel and adds one to a successful returned count. Thus the displayed run equality records a successful run initialized at y, with fuel and reported gradient-test count both N(y)+1, including the final successful test.\nFor these same witnesses, for w=((x,r),((u,v),(xi,xi-prime))) define\n  P0(w)=a r+sigma u,\n  Y0_i(w)=x+t_i P0(w),\n  Z0_j(w)=grad V(q(Y0_j(w))+sqrt(eta)xi_j),\n  Y1_i(w)=Y0_i(w)-sum_j omega_ij Z0_j(w),\n  Z1_j(w)=grad V(q(Y1_j(w))+sqrt(eta)xi-prime_j),\n  Phi(w)=(x+h P0(w)-sum_j b_j Z1_j(w),\n          a(P0(w)-sum_j d_j Z1_j(w))+sigma v).\nThen Phi is jointly measurable, and there exists a Markov kernel K from E x E to E x E such that for every incoming state s,\n  K(s)=(z -> Phi(s,z))_* gamma.\nIn addition, for every i in I, all of the following hold with integration over w under mu:\n  ||grad V(Y0_i)||^2 is integrable,\n  integral ||grad V(Y0_i)||^2 dmu <= 2 MR,\n  N(Y0_i)+1 is integrable,\n  integral (N(Y0_i)+1) dmu <= C[1+log(1+sqrt(2 MR)/epsilon)],\n  ||grad V(Y1_i)||^2 is integrable,\n  integral ||grad V(Y1_i)||^2 dmu <= 4 MR+2 S^2 B,\n  N(Y1_i)+1 is integrable,\n  integral (N(Y1_i)+1) dmu <= C[1+log(1+sqrt(4 MR+2 S^2 B)/epsilon)].\nCounts are cast to the reals in integrability and integral statements. Integrability is Bochner integrability, and all sums range over the entire finite index type. Each K(s) is a probability measure, with measurable dependence on s for every measurable output event. The theorem supplies these witnesses, the transition kernel, and these per-index input-gradient moment and interpreter-count bounds. It does not assert an invariant law, sampler convergence, final-state moment or bias bound, or an overall algorithmic gradient-call bound including the Z0 and Z1 evaluations.",
    "text_sha256": "7f437dc8a4b668da7c6dce02f0518724a55ea8e4a5a2966e18e4ed18ded890ad",
    "decoder": "anonymous_decoder_20261005",
